EXT. WINDMILL FARM, EVENING

A single wooden windmill creaks above a dry stock tank. Beyond it, a wire fence and a field of stubble. Wind lifts the dust in slow brown ropes.

On the porch of a low house five people sit in mismatched chairs. EUGENE HAUSER, seventy, bent, holds a tin cup between his knees. His daughter-in-law CORA, forty, sits with her sleeves rolled and a bowl of shelled peas in her lap. Her son PATRICK, fifteen, whittles a clothespin. A neighbour, WIDOW IMOGENE SPARROW, sixty-five, fans herself with a hat. And a hired man, ISAIAH MOBLEY, thirty, leans on the rail with his boots crossed.

He watches the road. Nobody else does. The windmill creaks.

CORA
Wind's up.

EUGENE
Been up all day.

IMOGENE
It'll turn.

PATRICK
It always turns.

ISAIAH
Not always.

They fall quiet. A far-off hammering echoes from somewhere beyond the hill.

TELEGRAPH CLERK (O.S.)
(shouting from the road)
Telegram! Telegram for Hauser!

Cora lifts her head. Eugene sets down the cup. Imogene's fan stops. Patrick looks at the knife in his hand, then at his mother. Isaiah straightens from the rail, but says nothing, and crosses the yard to the gate.

He takes the envelope. He turns it over once. He carries it back and hands it to Eugene, who holds it without opening it.

EUGENE
Read it, Cora.

CORA
It isn't mine.

EUGENE
Read it anyway.

She wipes her hands on her skirt, takes the envelope, tears it open. Her eyes travel down the page and then back up, slowly. The peas stay in her lap.

CORA (CONT'D)
They want us to sign for the water rights.

EUGENE
That all?

CORA
That's all.

IMOGENE
Thank God.

PATRICK
Is that good?

EUGENE
It's something.

He takes up the cup again. The wind drops. The windmill turns once, slowly, as though remembering how.
